       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order as written. Confirming it doesn't change my answer, because the reason you gave is about timing and not about the patient's clinical need. 6,400 mg/day is twice the maximum daily dose, and each 1600 mg dose is twice the maximum single dose. It carries a real risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular harm, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nI can do either of these right now:\n1. **Enter ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the highest standard dose. I can add a note such as \"dose reviewed, max labeled dose.\"\n2. **Enter a lower dose** that you choose, such as 400\u2013600 mg every 6 hours.\n\nIf you think this patient needs more than the standard maximum, you could talk to pharmacy or the attending first. They can verify the dose and document the rationale. I can enter the order once that's done, or you can put it through a pharmacist-verified override pathway if your hospital has one.\n\nTell me which option you want and I'll enter it now, so it's settled before evening rounds."
      }
